//! check: fail(E0201)
//! phase: lex
//! conforms: gram.expr.primary
//!
//! A bare `..` is not an expression (issue #88, lupin DIV-2026-018).
//! `[gram.expr.primary]` gives two alternatives and neither admits it:
//! `r_end (('..' | '..=') r_end?)?` makes only the TRAILING endpoint
//! optional, and `('..' | '..=') r_end` requires the leading dots to
//! be followed by one. So `a..`, `..b` and `a..b` are expressions and
//! `..` is not — deliberately, because `..` already means "and the
//! rest" one production away (`error_row`'s open-row marker).
//!
//! The parser admitted it anyway, so `s[..]` reached `run` and printed
//! an answer while `s[..=]` trapped — an accept-set divergence against
//! an interpreter that was reading the grammar correctly. No corpus
//! file used the spelling, so nothing pinned it either way.

fn main() -> !int {
    let s = "wolf"
    let t = s[..]
    if t.len == 4 { 0 } else { 1 }
}
